   "text": "nesformer\nAn NES CPU compiled into the weights of a stock Qwen3.5 model.\nThe NES's 6502 CPU (the core of its 2A03 chip), its 2 KB of work RAM, and a cartridge's program\nROM run inside a standard Qwen3_5ForCausalLM checkpoint. Nobody trained these weights; a\ncompiler computed every one. When you play a game, the model predicts the CPU's execution log\none token at a time. A small host program plays the rest of the console around it: it draws the\npicture, reads the controller, and fires the vblank interrupt.\nnesformer builds on Qwenputer by Divyansh Agrawal , which\ncompiled a CHIP-8 and then a RISC-V CPU into Qwen3.5-4B. It reuses Qwenputer's compiler and its\ncentral trick: the model's Gated DeltaNet state is the machine's memory.\nSuper Mario Bros. (World 1-1)\nFull Game by Doug Fraker (title screen and first level)\nEvery CPU token in these runs came from the model. For the bundled test games, every frame also\nmatches a conventional 6502 emulator's output exactly (see How it was checked ).\nQuick start\n1. Folder layout\nUnzip nesformer.zip and nesformer-weights.zip into the same folder. The weights go next to\nthe nesformer/ package, not inside it:\nnesformer/ <- run commands from here\n├── nesformer/ <- the Python package\n├── chipformer/ <- Qwenputer's compiler\n├── roms/ <- test ROMs (put your own here too)\n├── examples/ <- sample GIFs and frame sheets\n├── tests/\n├── LICENSE\n├── LICENSE.chipformer\n├── README.md\n├── pyproject.toml\n└── weights/ <- from nesformer-weights.zip\n├── config.json\n├── generation_config.json\n├── model.safetensors\n├── nesformer.json\n└── SHA256SUMS\nThe CLI looks for weights/ beside the nesformer/ package. To keep the weights elsewhere,\npass --weights /path/to/weights to run , check or trace , or give the path to info .\nTo confirm the weights unzipped correctly, run shasum -a 256 -c weights/SHA256SUMS from the\ntop folder.\n2. Install\nYou need Python 3.11 or newer.\npython3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate # Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate\npip install numpy scipy numba pillow\n3. Play a game\npython -m nesformer run roms/thwaite.nes --frames 600 --show \\\n--buttons \" start@90-95+200-205,a@230-233+250-253+270-273+290-293+310-313+330-333+350-353+370-373+390-393+410-413+430-433+450-453+470-473+490-493+510-513+530-533+550-553+570-573+590-593 \"\n--show draws each frame in your terminal as the model generates it. When the run ends, the CLI\nsaves an animated GIF named after the ROM (here thwaite.gif ) in the current folder.\nExpect it to be slow. The first run spends about 20 seconds compiling the engine with numba.\nAfter that, a frame takes 1–6 seconds, so 600 frames (10 seconds of NES time) take around\n25 minutes.\nPlaying your own ROMs\nPoint run at any .nes file that uses the NROM board (iNES mapper 0), such as Super Mario\nBros., Donkey Kong or Excitebike. You supply the ROM; nesformer only ships the three freely\nlicensed test ROMs in roms/ .\npython -m nesformer run roms/smb.nes --frames 600 --show \\\n--buttons \" start@60-65,right@200-600,a@250-265+300-315+360-375+420-435+480-495+540-555 \"\nThat script starts the game, then walks Mario right and jumps about once a second. The CLI loads\nthe ROM into the model's state at startup, so the same weights run any NROM game without a\nrebuild. Games on other boards stop with a message: see Limitations .\nController scripts\n--buttons takes a comma-separated list of entries. Each entry names one or more buttons joined\nby + , then @ , then one or more frame ranges joined by + :\nstart@60-65 press Start during frames 60 to 65\nright@200-600 hold Right from frame 200 to frame 600\na@250-265+300-315 press A twice\nright+b@200-600 hold Right and B together (run, in Super Mario Bros.)\nThe buttons are a , b , select , start , up , down , left and right . Frames count from\npower-on at 60 per second, so frame 600 is 10 seconds in. Games ignore input on screens where\nyou can't move, so holding a button a little early does no harm. In Super Mario Bros., a longer\nA press makes a higher jump.\nCommands\nCommand\nWhat it does\npython -m nesformer run ROM\nPlay a ROM on the weights and save a GIF\npython -m nesformer check ROM\nRun the weights and the reference CPU side by side; stop at the first difference\npython -m nesformer trace ROM\nPrint the model's token stream (default 40 tokens)\npython -m nesformer build\nCompile the circuit and write weights/ (about 10 seconds)\npython -m nesformer info\nDescribe the checkpoint\nOptions for run :\nOption\nDefault\nMeaning\n--frames N\n120\nStop after N frames\n--buttons SCRIPT\nnone\nController input (see above)\n--show\noff\nDraw frames in the terminal (needs 24-bit color, e.g. iTerm2 or Windows Terminal)\n--gif PATH\nROM name\nWhere to save the animated GIF\n--png DIR\noff\nSave each kept frame as a PNG in DIR. With --png alone, no GIF is saved. Add --gif to get both.\n--every N\n1\nKeep every N-th frame\n--scale N\n2\nEnlarge saved frames N times\n--engine\nfast\nfast (numba), numpy , or hf (stock transformers; needs torch, ~10 tokens/s)\n--no-skip\noff\nRun every iteration of idle wait loops (slower; identical frames)\n--max-tokens N\nnone\nStop after N tokens\n--trace FILE\nnone\nSave the token ids\nrun , check and trace all accept --weights , --engine , --buttons and --no-skip .\ncheck --tokens N sets how far to compare (default 50,000). trace --tokens N sets how many\ntokens to print (default 40). build --out DIR writes the checkpoint somewhere other than\nweights/ .\nHow it works\nWhat's in the weights, and what isn't\nIn the model (weights + DeltaNet state)\nIn the host ( nesformer/host.py )\nThe 6502 core: all 151 official opcodes, every addressing mode, flags, stack, BRK and NMI entry\nThe PPU: a scanline renderer and the palette\n2 KB of work RAM\nControllers; APU registers (written, otherwise ignored)\n32 KB of PRG-ROM, stored twice: one copy for instruction fetch, one for data reads\nNMI timing at vblank, injected as an NMI token\nOAM DMA reads (the 256 bytes appear as DM tokens)\nAnswers to I/O reads, injected as IN tokens\nThis split follows Qwenputer's CHIP-8 build, wh"
