   "text": "Home\nHow Do You Take a Picture of a Black Hole?\nRead Later\nShare\nCopied!\nComments\nRead Later\nRead Later\nThe Joy of Why\nHow Do You Take a Picture of a Black Hole?\nBy\nJanna Levin\nand\nSteven Strogatz\nOctober 29, 2026\nAstrophysicist Shep Doeleman explains how capturing the first image of a black hole required a telescope the size of the world, and why the next goal is to create a movie.\nRead Later\nAdd as Preferred Source\n(opens in a new tab)\ni\nClick to tell Google to prioritize Quanta and see more of the best coverage of science and math in your search and AI results.\nChanelle Nibbelink for Quanta Magazine\nIntroduction\nBlack holes are one of the universe’s most exotic and elusive objects. When nothing, not even light, can escape their powerful gravitational grasp, taking a picture of one seems unimaginable. But an image of a black hole, or more accurately its shadow, could offer insights that theory alone may not provide when testing predictions. So how can it be done?\nHarvard astrophysicist Shep Doeleman and a team of 250 researchers achieved this extraordinary feat by linking a group of radio telescopes around the world. This network, called the Event Horizon Telescope , produced the first image of a supermassive black hole in the galaxy M87 , around 55 million light-years away, and the first image of the black hole at the heart of our galaxy . In this episode, Doeleman tells co-host Janna Levin how he turned the Earth into a telescope capable of seeing a black hole, the incredible level of science and collaboration that was required to achieve this, how the images have furthered our understanding of these exotic objects, and how movies of black holes may be possible by the early 2030s.\nListen on Apple Podcasts , Spotify , TuneIn or your favorite podcasting app, or you can stream it from Quanta .\nTranscript\nJANNA LEVIN: Okay. I’m Janna Levin\nSTEVE STROGATZ: And I’m Steve Strogatz.\nLEVIN: And this is The Joy of Why\nSTROGATZ: A podcast from Quanta Magazine where we look at some of the biggest unanswered questions in math and science today.\nLEVIN: Welcome to another episode.\nSTROGATZ: Great to see you.\nLEVIN: Great to see you. I’m pretty excited about this one. I cannot deny or hide it. We’re talking about not only an area where I’ve spent a lot of my life black holes — the greatest, strangest topic out there — but also I had a chance to talk to an old friend. So it’s a fun episode, I hope.\nSTROGATZ: I’m really looking forward to hearing it. I know you’ve always had a special affection for black holes. I’ve had a chance to interview you in some earlier version of this.\nLEVIN: That’s right.\nSTROGATZ: And you blew my mind over and over about black holes.\nLEVIN: Yeah, they require some defense. They’ve got a bad reputation, not entirely earned.\nSTROGATZ: What’s the bad reputation? Because they like to eat things?\nLEVIN: Yeah. You know, black holes, kind of this monster truck language around black holes all the time. You know, they’re like… But actually, black holes are remarkably elegant, clean, simple systems. They leave you alone if you leave them alone. They’re actually quite small for their heft. That’s kind of their whole gestalt, that they’re actually tiny for their heft.\nAnd, uh, you know, though they’re the darkest phenomenon in the universe, they figure out a way to become the brightest beacons that we see on the edge of what’s observable.\nSTROGATZ: Wow.\nLEVIN: So, they’re, they’re pretty awesome. I also think that they’re fundamental to physics, unlike any other macroscopic object you can think of.\nSTROGATZ: Hmmm. When I think of black holes, I tend to focus on what they do to spacetime. You know, that they cause singularities. It’s their geometry that always grabs me. But that’s not really necessarily the only way to think about black holes. They’re really objects. Real players.\nLEVIN: Yeah. I think after people got over the thrill that they actually existed out there and that they could indirectly be detected, there was a sense that they’re not major players in the universe. “Eh, so there’s some black holes out there.”\nQuite to the contrary, I think we’ve come to understand that they sculpt our history and our past, that they have a very regulating and important role in determining the shape and size of galaxies and where there might be habitable regions even. And ultimately, we’re kinda doomed to fall into black holes eventually. So, they’re kind of our past and our future.\nBut, it might surprise people that we had never actually taken a picture of a black hole. All of our evidence had been indirect until about 10 years ago, and that group worked for well over a decade, for some people up to two decades, to try to capture the first picture of a black hole.\nSTROGATZ: Huh. Wild.\nLEVIN: It was a tremendous accomplishment, led by Shep Doeleman, who is our guest. So let me tell you about Shep. Shep is a senior research fellow at Harvard University, the assistant director of Harvard’s Black Hole Initiative, also an astrophysicist at the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory.\nAnd he was the founding director of the Event Horizon Telescope Consortium, which is a network of observatories around the world which gave us the first and only images humanity has ever captured of black holes.\nSTROGATZ: Hmm. This sounds like it’s gonna be a great discussion of science at its best.\nLEVIN: Yeah, it’s an epic story. Let’s hear from Shep himself.\n[ Music plays ]\nLEVIN: Shep, welcome to The Joy of Why .\nSHEP DOELEMAN: It’s good to be here, Janna.\nLEVIN: It’s a pleasure to have you on the show. I’m really curious if you remember not just your way into black holes, but your way into the entire idea of thinking about the universe?\nDOELEMAN: Wow. Well, everybody falls in love with astronomy at some point. I don’t care who you are, you fall in love with astronomy. And whether it’s staring up at the night sky, you know, at summer camp or seeing a meteorite, for me, it was seeing a total solar eclipse. I was 13"
