   "text": "The breach didn't appear to affect Polymarket’s U.S.-based platform. Gabby Jones / Bloomberg via Getty\nShare\nAdd NBC News to Google\nOct. 29, 2026, 3:09 PM EDT / Updated Oct. 29, 2026, 3:23 PM EDT\nBy Rob Wile\nPrediction markets platform Polymarket was forced to halt trading on its international platform Thursday for nearly an hour because of what it said was a compromise of third-party software.\nThere were also unconfirmed reports from users on Polymarket’s Discord channel who said their wallets had been drained.\nWhile Polymarket did not confirm that users had lost money, it said on X post “anyone who has been impacted will be made whole.”\n“We’ll make this right and are fully reimbursing all affected traders,” the company said in a subsequent post on X .\nPolymarket said the attack appeared to emanate from the United Kingdom and that it had referred the matter to British law enforcement authorities.\nThe hackers gained access to the funds via software from DebugBear, a web analytics and website performance tool, according to a person familiar with the details who was granted anonymity to address an evolving security situation.\nDebugBear said on X that it was experiencing an “ongoing security incident” at around 2 p.m. ET Thursday.\nCrypto news website The Block reported that some $2.65 million in cryptocurrencies had been siphoned from Polymarket users’ wallets, citing data from blockchain analysis group Arkham.\nA Polymarket representative declined to comment on that figure or on the precise nature of the attack.\nPolymarket said in its X post that it had identified and fixed an issue affecting its international platform, Polymarket.com.\n“One of our 3rd party providers was compromised, and we have referred this to relevant law enforcement,” Polymarket said.\n“Out of an abundance of caution, we have temporarily halted trading & are working to bring the platform back online,” it added.\nFor Subscribers\nFOR SUBSCRIBERS Why broadcaster Ernie Johns