RIBERS GOP former Sen. John Sununu seeks distance from Trump in New Hampshire comeback bid\nFOR SUBSCRIBERS Ohio legislators alarmed by Medicaid fraud crackdown’s impact on disabled people\nFOR SUBSCRIBERS Top general fired by Hegseth amid leak investigation\nFOR SUBSCRIBERS Trump pushes his administration to ramp up investigations into the Alaska Senate race\nFOR SUBSCRIBERS An ugly, Islamophobic turn in Michigan’s Senate race\nFOR SUBSCRIBERS Chinese hackers used DeepSeek and other AI tools in sweeping global cyberattacks, report finds\nFOR SUBSCRIBERS Israel’s Arab parties emerge from election as kingmakers and a focus of anger\nFOR SUBSCRIBERS Cruise company had troubled history before abandoning passengers in Cambodia\nFOR SUBSCRIBERS Dark money group seeks to undercut Republican candidates with pro-gun voters\nSlide 1 of 1\nPolymarket’s U.S.-based platform did not appear to be affected by the breach.\nCompromising third-party vendors has become a common way malicious actors gain access to large platforms and websites.\nThese hackers’ success in stealing money from Polymarket users, however, makes this a significant breach.\nPolymarket was valued at $21 billion in September after a group of investors led by Donald Trump Jr. invested $1 billion in the platform.\nThe company processes about $200 million worth of trading activity daily.\nPolymarket, along with rival Kalshi, has become the subject of an intense, multimillion-dollar battle between U.S. states and the Trump administration over who gets to regulate the prediction-market betting that occurs on the sites.\nThe platforms also continue to fight off potential insider trading activity. Earlier Thursday, Bloomberg News reported Polymarket was investigating reports of suspicious bets made amid Israel’s closely watched elections.\nIt is also investigating “dozens” of bets related to the U.S. midterms, according to the company’s head of global intelligence and investigations, Shana Bautista.\nEarlier this month, Kalshi said it was looking into suspicious bets involving Trump’s choi"
