   "text": "A customer asked for help writing a batch file to launch a program. For some reason, the program displayed an error message when the batch file ran it, whereas it worked fine when they double-clicked the program from Explorer.\nI immediately suspected that they were not setting the current directory.\nSome time ago, I noted that when you double-click a file in Explorer, Explorer sets the current directory of the program to the directory containing the thing that you double-clicked . This is done to accomodate programs that look for ancillary files in the current directory. The example I had was a hypothetical LitWare Writer program that, when given an ABC.LIT file, looks for related files like ABC.LTC (the table of contents) and ABC.LDC (the custom dictionary) in the current directory.\nThis also happens if you double-click the .exe file: The current directory is set to the directory that contains the executable.\nMy guess is that the customer’s program looks for ancillary files relative to its current directory, but the batch file ran the program by its full path without changing the current directory to match the program’s path.\nThe customer confirmed that explicitly changing the directory fixed the program. (More specifically, they used pushd to change the directory and then popd to restore the original directory.)\nBonus chatter : It didn’t come to it, but my second idea would have been to check whether the program had set a custom entry under App Paths to ask for additional directories to be added to the PATH environment variable when the program is run.\n9\n0\nCategory\nOld New Thing\nTopics\nOther\nShare\nAuthor\nRaymond Chen\nRaymond has been involved in the evolution of Windows for more than 30 years. In 2003, he began a Web site known as The Old New Thing which has grown in popularity far beyond his wildest imagination, a development which still gives him the heebie-jeebies. The Web site spawned a book, coincidentally also titled The Old New Thing (Addison Wesley 2007). He occasionally appears on the Windows Dev Docs Twitter account to tell stories which convey no useful information.\n9 comments\nJoin the discussion.\nLeave a comment Cancel reply\nSign in\nSort by :\nNewest\nNewest\nPopular\nOldest\nI was expecting some weird case of a GUI app trying to alloc its own console window.. but failing when it already inherited one.\nI think I’ve seen that happen, but I can’t remember the details off hand.\nI long for a day when a modern operating system does not have any concept of a “current directory”. Global mutable state with security implications. At least UWP fixed the “mutable” and “security” parts. Still don’t know why they thought Windows Terminal needed to be able to change the classical working directory global instead of using their own…\nIt’s process scope, so it is debatable if you want to call it global or not.\nFrom the perspective of multiple threads running in the process, the working directory could be changed at any time by some other thread, and the Win32 API function for retrieving the current working directory warns about this.\nThere are many ways to specify the absolute path of an exe before starting it, but doing so for a dll linked via a lib is much harder, or even infeasible.\nWindows lacks a way to specify dll load paths in the manifest (for example, searching for a dll relative to itself). This means that when exes is in different directories, the same dll must also be copied into those directories. This prevents many dlls from being shared. Hardlinks are one workaround, but I don't consider it ideal. Windows does have such mechanisms, such as mui and side-by-side, but they...\nRead more There are many ways to specify the absolute path of an exe before starting it, but doing so for a dll linked via a lib is much harder, or even infeasible.\nWindows lacks a way to specify dll load paths in the manifest (for example, searching for a dll relative to itself). This means that when exes is in different directories, the same dll must also be copied into those directories. This prevents many dlls from being shared. Hardlinks are one workaround, but I don’t consider it ideal. Windows does have such mechanisms, such as mui and side-by-side, but they are all used to select different dlls and are very limited in scope, rather than being used to share dlls.\nRead less\nThis is why my programs, when they start, ask the system for the path to the file that’s been run, and then uses that to reference other files it expects in the same directory.\nGetModuleFileName to path\nDrop leaf from path\nload path+”otherfile”\nload path+”anotherfile”\nload path+”someotherfile”\netc.\nI thought this was obvious, it’s been obvious to me since the early 1980s, it never occured to me to do it any other way.\nFor my MSIX apps in the MS Store, I've just gone like the other posters said and have my application aware of its own environment. My startup basically says, \"what's my current exe, am I in debug, or not, and let's go find out where our data lives in localappdata.\n<code>\nRead more For my MSIX apps in the MS Store, I’ve just gone like the other posters said and have my application aware of its own environment. My startup basically says, “what’s my current exe, am I in debug, or not, and let’s go find out where our data lives in localappdata.\nvoid copy_if_newer(const std::filesystem::path& source, const std::filesystem::path& destination)\n{\nif (!std::filesystem::exists(destination))\n{\nstd::filesystem::create_directories(destination);\nstd::filesystem::copy(source, destination, std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing | std::filesystem::copy_options::recursive);\n}\nelse if (developer_mode || std::filesystem::last_write_time(source) > std::filesystem::last_write_time(destination))\n{\nstd::filesystem::copy(source, destination, std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing | std::filesystem::copy_options::recursive);\n}\n}\nvoid my_app(HINSTANCE hInstance, LPSTR lpszCmdParam)\n{\ncorona::corona_db_read_only = false;\nstd::sh"
  },
