   "text": "October 27, 2026 Finding panics with Call Graph Analyzer\nWouter\nEmbedded software consultant\nopen-source rust tools\nWe often get asked \"How can I eliminate panics in my Rust code?\" There have been several attempts to tackle this issue. Under the Open Device Partnership and working together with Microsoft, we have developed and released the Call Graph Analyzer (CGA) , a tool to extract the callgraph from monomorphized MIR . This callgraph can be used to track down what functions in the compiled binary can panic.\nWe often get asked \"How can I eliminate panics in my Rust code?\" There have been several attempts to tackle this issue. Under the Open Device Partnership and working together with Microsoft, we have developed and released the Call Graph Analyzer (CGA) , a tool to extract the callgraph from monomorphized MIR . This callgraph can be used to track down what functions in the compiled binary can panic.\nFor example, when you run cargo cga check on your code, you get:\nerror: call to method `core::num::<impl u64>::strict_sub` which can panic\n--> src/main.rs:18:17\n|\n18 | let _ = 2u64.strict_sub(u64::MAX);\n| ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nIn this blogpost we discuss why this new approach is both better and worse than pre-existing approaches at finding panics.\nWhy eliminate panics though?\nBefore discussing the Call Graph Analyzer, we should first consider why panics are useful, and in what cases it might be beneficial to minimize or perhaps even completely eliminate any possible panic. This topic has been explored extensively by others [ 1 ][ 2 ][ 3 ][ 4 ], but it is useful to summarize here. Lets start with a brief description:\nRust provides a mechanism to prevent a function from returning normally, and instead “panic,” which is a response to an error condition that is typically not expected to be recoverable ...\nThe Rust Reference - Panic\nThese unrecoverable error conditions may either be caused by a bug in the implementation of a function, or constitute violated entry conditions for a function (so a bug in the function itself or a mistake by the caller of that function).\nAn example is calling 1.strict_add(u32::MAX) , which would result in a panic in response to the caller passing invalid arguments to the operation. Similarly a normal addition 1 + u32::MAX in Rust will panic if overflow-checks are enabled.\nInstead of panicking, error conditions can be dealt with by:\nmaking them recoverable by having the function return a Result<T, E> or Option<T> instead. For addition this is provided by u32::checked_add .\nmaking the function fail implicitly or silently and describing that behaviour in the function documentation. Arithmetic operations can for example wrap around with u32::wrapping_add .\nmaking a function never return, e.g. using an infinite loop {} . Sometimes this approach is accidentally used when employing a retry scheme without a timeout or maximum iteration count.\nHowever striving for zero panics is very hard and quite foolish: code is rarely void of assumptions. Returning Result<T, AssumptionViolated> everywhere just pushes the burden up the call stack, until you will abort anyway at the root. This essentially yields the same behaviour as if you were to panic.\nIt is useful to make panics explicit though. It is better to know about your panic paths and to reason about them, than to just accidentally include panics and ignore that they exist. Having regression analysis on your panic paths is also very useful, notifying you of any new panic paths introduced in your code.\nHaving said that, in the following cases it might be justified to eliminate some of your panics:\nIn contexts where code size really matters, like on embedded devices. Panics result in a relatively large amount of code due to the required strings and associated code to format these strings. Instead, we can: use defmt::panic to reduce this string bloat. This requires all panics to be wrapped in the macro. For library dependencies this is typically only done when the library is specifically intended to be used on an embedded platform. Defmt is not used in other libraries, including the standard library of Rust.\nuse -Cpanic=immediate-abort to prevent panics from being emitted entirely and instead just crash the application. This does not leave a lot of information that can be used for debugging though.\nIn critical code paths that must absolutely never fail, for example: an externally accessible service that needs to prevent a Denial of Service (DOS) attack which crashes the service. Invalid input should result in a user-facing error instead.\nthe firmware update path on an embedded device, including the bootloader.\nIn panic handling code itself. In Rust when unwinding a panic in an implementation of Drop causes a so-called 'double-drop' and may cause the program to abort. Secondly a panic in a custom #[panic_handler] is not allowed, but it is unclear what should happen then .\nStriving for no unrecoverable error conditions is extremely hard in practice. Generally it is better to implement top-level recovery mechanisms. In embedded this would entail using a Watchdog timer which resets the device if it is stuck, and a recovery image to restore the device to a working state. On a host machine this could mean catching unwinding panics using catch_unwind . Note that the latter is not fool-proof and comes with a few caveats. Externally accessible services should be redundant and fast to recover.\nIn conclusion, it is useful to find unintentional panics.\nPrior art\nCurrently, ways to extract callgraphs or detect panics include:\nrustig (ELF debug info analysis)\nfindpanics (ELF debug info analysis)\ncargo-callgraph (Rust HIR analyzer)\ncargo-callgraph (rust-analyzer based exporter)\ncharon (Rust MIR exporter)\nno-panic (Linker-based panic detection)\ndont_panic (Linker-based panic detection)\nYou can download any of these projects, and with the exception of Charon (which is not explicitly meant for this usecase) it is very easy to get started.\nLin"
  },
