   "text": "Geist started as a monospace typeface for developers, built for the editors and terminals where they spend their day doing their work. From there it grew into Geist Sans for product interfaces, and then into Geist Pixel, which was inspired by the grids of early computer screens.\nToday, we're adding a new member to the family: Geist Serif .\nGeist Serif posters\nGeist Serif is a high-contrast display serif built on the same foundations as Geist Sans, Geist Mono, and Geist Pixel. It has fine, saw-toothed serifs and sharp hairlines, and it was drawn to communicate messages powerfully both in the physical world and on the high-density screens on modern devices.\nCopy link to heading Same system, new voice\nAt Vercel, Geist Sans runs the product. For headlines, though, we wanted a second voice, one that belongs to the same system, instead of a stranger picked from a drop-down menu.\nA lot of tech brands have moved away from clean sans-serifs to get some personality back, and many of them borrow from vintage computer ads. Geist Serif takes a different path: toward fashion, editorial design, and magazine headlines. It's our homage to the artists behind Rolling Stone and Harper's Bazaar .\nFrom the beginning, each Geist family has read from a chapter of type history: the Swiss design movement, early terminal and DOS computing, and now editorial print.\nCopy link to heading Display first\nGeist Serif began as a set of sketches by Evil Rabbit , which Andrés Briganti then expanded. The first letters already showed an expressive serif, in the spirit of typefaces like Victor Serif and Stanley.\nOriginal Geist Serif drawings\nWhen Mark Davis took over the drawings, we made the first release a display face for headlines, posters, and other large sizes, while work on the full family continued. Print has always struggled to hold fine hairlines and sharp serifs, but today's displays can handle a high level of detail. We wanted the new face to take full advantage of modern screens, so we thinned every downstroke by 50% and tightened the sidebearings to push the contrast as far as it would go.\nEach serif has a wedge on one side and a curve on the other, so the proportions are classical and look beautiful in print, but the construction is new and optimized for digital devices.\n“ We created something that could only be made for today. Not overly blocky for screens from a decade ago, not filled with mechanical compromises for physical ink. Just serifs at perfect points. ”\nMark Davis founder & font designer at WORDMARK\nCopy link to heading From the first prototype to today\nThe first prototype of Geist Serif was shared on April 1, 2026. Here's the same word, Geist, in that prototype, compared with the display drawing shipping today.\nOriginal prototype shown as dotted lines\nCopy link to heading Geist grew teeth\nThe goal was to honor our original intent and keep the serif closely tied to the sans, even when the letters sit on top of each other. Geist Serif’s stems match Geist Sans, and the two share widths and postures.\nThe saw-toothed serifs and high contrast give Geist an editorial voice. One phrase kept coming up as each character was drawn: “Geist grew teeth.”\nCopy link to heading Four families, one x-height\nGeist Serif uses the same cap height and x-height as Geist Sans, so you can drop it into any Geist layout without moving a line. Mix Sans, Mono, Pixel, and Serif in the same sentence and every word looks the same size.\nMetric\nGeist Sans\nGeist Serif\nUPM\n1000\n1000\nCap height\n710\n710\nx-height\n530\n530\nAscender\n710\n750\nDescender\n-150\n-200\nCopy link to heading Four variants, one axis\nLike Geist Pixel, Geist Serif has a variable axis that swaps the shape of every dot in the font, including those on i and j and in punctuation. The axis is called Element Shape ( ELSH ), and it gives you four variants: Regular with round dots (the default), Square, Triangle (a nod to the Vercel logo), and Heart.\nEach variant also ships as its own static file. If you use the variable font, pick a variant with font-variation-settings :\nCopy link to heading Crafted by hand\nIn July the font had 227 glyphs . It now has 894 and covers 352 Latin-script languages, and we worked through more than 52,000 kerning pairs to get the spacing right. Some of the details are tiny. The eye of the g , for example, is tilted exactly 7°.\nCopy link to heading Specs at a glance\nGeist Serif ships with:\n1 weight (Regular)\n4 variants (Regular, Square, Triangle, Heart)\n894 glyphs\n5 stylistic sets ( ss01 to ss05 )\n352 supported languages\nCopy link to heading Getting started\nGeist Serif ships in the same package as the rest of the family:\nterminal\n1 npm i geist\nExports and CSS variables:\nGeistSerif (variable, all shapes): --font-geist-serif\nGeistSerifRegular : --font-geist-serif-regular\nGeistSerifSquare : --font-geist-serif-square\nGeistSerifTriangle : --font-geist-serif-triangle\nGeistSerifHeart : --font-geist-serif-heart\nEach one has its own entry point, so you only ship the font file you use. For example, in layout.tsx with GeistSerifRegular :\n1 import { GeistSerifRegular } from 'geist/font/serif/regular' ;\n2\n3 export default function RootLayout ( { children } ) {\n4 return (\n5 < html lang = \" en \" className = { GeistSerifRegular . variable } >\n6 < body > { children } </ body >\n7 </ html >\n8 ) ;\n9 }\nYou can also download the font files, test every stylistic set, and inspect each glyph at vercel.com/font .\nCopy link to heading What’s next\nRegular is the first style we’re releasing, and the rest of the family is already in progress. We’re testing future versions while building this one, so this release is just the start.\nCopy link to heading Weights from Thin to Black\nThe variable font will range from Thin (100) to Black (900). In this week’s proofs, the g in Black is the letter we can’t stop looking at.\nCopy link to heading Toward a text face\nTo explore a text version, we built a foundry tool that measures strokes, including where they meet, and thickens the display outlin"
  },
