   "title": "Voyager 1 gives controllers the silent treatment",
   "url": "https://www.theregister.com/science/2026/10/30/voyager-1-gives-controllers-the-silent-treatment/5306637",
   "discussUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50306433",
   "points": 10,
   "comments": 1,
   "published": "2026-10-31T01:39:22+09:00",
   "seen": false,
   "text": "science\nVoyager 1 gives controllers the silent treatment\nWhew! Only kidding, here I am\nRichard Speed\nRichard\nSpeed\nMICROSOFT ECOSYSTEM REPORTER\nPublished\nfri 30 Oct 2026 // 15:30 UTC\nMake us preferred on Google\nREAD MORE\nNASA ponders a Hubble servicing mission or a controlled deorbit\n2 hours ago\nBoffin's software found a possible dwarf planet - a result so rare he thought it must be a bug\n1 day ago\nPlease state the nature of the medical emergency: Google is building a robot medic for NASA\n1 day ago\nChina's spaceplane bears striking resemblance to America's X-37B\n2 days ago\nOpen Cosmos steps up Europe's space sovereignty game, shows off ConnectedCosmos satellites\n2 days ago\nVoyager 1 gave controllers the silent treatment for a few days this month before resuming communication weeks before the spacecraft reaches the one-light-day milestone.\nThe incident happened on October 18: NASA's Deep Space Network (DSN) couldn't detect a signal from the probe. On October 19, on the next scheduled communication attempt, there was still nothing. Was this it? Had the decades-long galactic venture finally ended?\nThings are operating on a knife's edge for the two veteran spacecraft, which will reach their half-century next year. Voyager 2 suffered an antenna pointing issue in 2023, and a low power condition caused Voyager 1 to unexpectedly turn off one of its two radio transmitters.\nREG AD\nThe good news is that on October 20, the signal reappeared, leaving engineers wondering why Voyager 1 temporarily went silent.\nREG AD\nThus far, it seems a group of thrusters went offline and their backups were turned on. This could indicate onboard computers detected a problem and put the spacecraft into a safe mode.\nKareem Badaruddin, Voyager mission manager at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, said: \"This is the first time we've seen the spacecraft do something like this, but we are also at a point in the mission where we have to expect the unexpected.\n\"We're relieved to be back in contact with Voyager 1 and are eager to understand what happened.\"\nThe Register spoke to Badaruddin in 2024 , where he described the challenges of working with the veteran spacecraft, but he reckoned the work was the \"best job at JPL.\"\nThe twin Voyager spacecraft have recently been through a procedure dubbed the \"Big Bang,\" where controllers turned off onboard devices and switched to lower power alternatives. The result will extend the lifetime of the spacecraft by a few more years and postpone further instrument deactivation. It was completed first on Voyager 2, and then Voyager 1, six weeks later in September.\nHowever, the brief lapse in communication highlights that despite the ingenuity of the team that keep the spacecraft running, the project must eventually come to an end. In 2026, Suzanne Dodd, Project Manager for the Voyager project, told The Register what the end would look like .\nShe said, \"We're just going to one day not hear the signal from the DSN, and we're going to rush around and make sure that it's not something on the ground.\"\nMonths of listening will follow, before engineers throw in the towel and declare the mission over. And then? Maybe a farewell message sent to the last known location.\nREG AD\nThankfully, in this case, the signal returned, so no need for a goodbye transmission just yet.\nIn a few weeks, Voyager 1 will be so far away that it will take a full 24 hours for commands to reach it and another 24 hours for a response to reach Earth.\nCommunications blip notwithstanding, there is every chance both Voyager spacecraft will still be operational as they pass their half-century milestone next year and, technology and funding permitting, continue sending data to Earth for a good few years yet. ®\nvoyager\nnasa\nscience\nspace\nMake us preferred on Google\nREG AD\nGM cracks the petty cash box for multistate driver tracking settlement\n$220 million? For all of you? Yeah, we can probably dig that up ...\nAnthropic: That Claude Code extension compromise… couldn't be us\nClaudeshop fingers 'malicious actor' in targeted supply-chain attack on Cursor\nBuilt to bend: Postgres’s inventor and a steward on what AI really needs\nPARTNER CONTENT: Michael Stonebraker and Bruce Momjian on why enterprise AI needs a database that no one controls, and what PostgreSQL 19 says about the road ahead\nVoyager 1 gives controllers the silent treatment\nWhew! Only kidding, here I am\nPHP2Shell is a warning shot for vulnerability disclosure\nKeeping secrets is difficult in an open world, but we've done it before and must do it again\nAlpineBSD announced – an AI-free FOSS OS\nCombining the NetBSD kernel with the Alpine Linux userland"
