   "title": "Show HN: Fraktur, a DJ mixing app in the browser",
   "url": "https://greenvitriol.com/posts/fraktur",
   "discussUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50303832",
   "points": 13,
   "comments": 0,
   "published": "2026-10-30T22:47:49+09:00",
   "seen": false,
   "text": "TL;DR - I made a DJ mixing app. You can find the web version at fraktur.hya.io .\nFraktur: A DJ mixing app in the browser\nSome of you might know that I used to DJ at a lot of parties and festivals about 15-20 years ago. For some random reason, I found myself looking at the DJ software scene recently, and let me tell you, it feels different from 2008. Some apps I liked are discontinued; others still exist, but their parent company is at risk of going out of business. Most now require a monthly subscription fee and an internet connection, have a street/urban aesthetic aimed at younger audiences and try to sell you beat packs at every turn. I'm sure this is the only business model that makes sense in this recurring revenue economy, and I'm not really hating on them (except for the urban vibe and the beat packs, come on), but, to be honest, I can't be arsed.\n(There's also an open-source app called Mixxx . It's not really for me, but if you want a free, battle-tested DJ app, consider it.)\nSo, this being the golden age of personal software, I decided to build my own DJ mixing app with the Web Audio API and Electron (plus a bit of Rust), a move I'm sure will be regarded as a bad idea and will make people very angry. In my defence, the Web Audio API with Audio Worklets is quite efficient these days (gone are the days of ScriptProcessorNode ), and with a fixed number of decks you can bound memory consumption, allocate one track's audio buffer per deck and evict its buffer after it has played live (more on that later).\nIn further defence, this app is for personal use. I don't think I'll ever put myself through the pain of properly distributing and supporting it, so nobody will be able to install it and complain that it would have used less memory had I written it in C99.\nBut hey, since Electron is a web app and IndexedDB has good storage capabilities and we've been able to compile Rust to WASM for a while, it wasn't really hard to extract an entirely web-based app from the code. The web app is fully featured and can be found at fraktur.hya.io .\n(btw: Fraktur is a pun on the first VST plugin I created as a young developer , which had a heavy blackletter logo, and also a pun on the music app scene of the late 2000s, when we would go clubbing in Berlin and every good-sounding plugin had a German name)\nIndustrial-goth design was big in 2006\nHow a DJ mixing app works, simplified\nPremise: here I'm specifically talking about club DJs, people who stand behind a console near the dance floor and play music for other people to dance or mosh to. The main task of a club DJ is to take two tracks with similar tempos and synchronize them by changing the playback speed of the incoming track and aligning the bass drums, a process called beatmatching . Once two tracks are beatmatched, they can play together for a long while and fade into each other nicely, the idea being to transition seamlessly between them so people can dance without interruption. There are many tricks you can do to make this process more elaborate, but that's pretty much the gist of it.\nIn my experience, there are two main types of DJ mixing software: one where you play track B over track A by pressing the \"play\" button at some point to start track B live, and a less common one where you anchor track B to track A in advance, like audio lanes in a DAW . I spent a long time inside DAWs in my youth, so I personally prefer the second type, and Fraktur is built around this premise.\nTracks lined up in Fraktur, with the pre-listen playhead set near the anchor point\nPlayheads\nThe main difference between a DAW and a DJ mixing app is that a DAW normally has a single playhead, while a DJ mixing app has two: one live and one for pre-listening. The live playhead only moves forward: it's the uninterrupted stream of music the dance floor hears. The pre-listen playhead corresponds to what the DJ hears in the headphones and can be moved around to monitor the mix in advance.\nFor this reason, a DJ using mixing software needs an audio interface with at least two stereo outputs or two audio interfaces with a stereo output each, one dedicated to the live playhead and one to the headphone pre-listen output (there used to be very lo-fi setups where you would route the live output to the right channel and the pre-listen output to the left channel at the expense of stereo sound, but technology has made them obsolete). Fraktur needs two outputs to work live, but the web app starts in pre-listen-only mode so you can learn the controls without configuring your outputs.\nPlease note there is a bug in Chromium on Mac that doesn't let you access channels > 2 via Web Audio, which should be fixed in version 156, so you might want to update your browser. Safari and Firefox don't seem to support explicit routing yet. You'll at least be able to use pre-listen mode in all these cases.\nLive and pre-listen playheads playing together in a live session\n(By the way, if you don't have a fancy sound card and all you want is to record your mix, projects like Blackhole for Mac let you create a virtual sound card that you can use as your live output.)\nThe audio configuration dialog\nDecks\nFraktur has 4 decks. One deck can hold one track. You can split tracks into pieces and copy-paste them around to repeat a section during a mix. That still counts as a track that lives in a deck. When the live playhead passes the last piece of a track, its deck clears automatically, ready for another one.\nThe decks are used in sequence. A session starts with the DJ loading a track into Deck A and starting live playback, then loading a different track into Deck B and preparing the mix using the pre-listen output. The DJ can then load an additional track and prepare the next mix in Deck C, and the one after that in Deck D. At some point during this process, the live playhead will reach the end of the track in Deck A and the deck will be cleared automatically, ready to be reused, and so on. This guarantees that a ma"
