   "text": "Insights\nOctober 29, 2026\nNorth Korea Steals USD 6.36 Billion From Binance in the Largest Hack in History\nTRM Team\nKey takeaways\nOn October 29, 2026, an attacker moved approximately USD 6.36 billion out of Binance wallets on ten blockchains, most of it in about 20 minutes starting at 20:05 UTC. That makes this the largest crypto theft on record, about four times the USD 1.5 billion Bybit hack of February 2025. Binance has confirmed a security incident and suspended withdrawals on several networks.\nTRM attributes the attack to TraderTraitor, North Korea’s elite hacking unit, based on robust on and off-chain intelligence. We make this judgment with high confidence. TRM is tracking the funds and alerting partners through the Beacon Network.\nOn Ethereum, the attacker used an EIP-7702 delegation to turn a Binance hot wallet into a sweeper it controlled, with 50 pre-signed authorizations prepared in advance. In the same window, Binance wallets on the other nine chains sent funds to the attacker through ordinary signed transfers. In TRM's assessment, this points to a compromise of Binance's hot wallet signing infrastructure.\nTether, Circle, Ripple and World Liberty Financial froze about USD 2.67 billion in stablecoins, and Arbitrum's Security Council froze 8,426 ETH and 74.6 million ARB on its network, for a total of roughly USD 2.7 billion. The attacker raced the freezes by selling USDT to market makers and borrowing ETH against USDT on Aave. It now holds more than 420,000 ETH and 18,270 BTC, about USD 2.4 billion in assets beyond any issuer's reach.\nWhat happened\nOn the evening of October 29, billions of dollars in crypto began leaving wallets belonging to Binance, the world's largest cryptocurrency exchange. Within minutes, Binance wallets on the XRP Ledger, Ethereum, Bitcoin, Tron, Solana and four other networks had sent their balances to addresses controlled by a single attacker. A second wave around 23:05 UTC reached Binance wallets on Avalanche and Polygon.\nBinance said it was investigating a potential security issue, then said it had temporarily suspended withdrawals on multiple networks as a precaution. Binance has said user funds are safe. The story is still developing, and the figures here reflect what TRM saw onchain as of October 30 at 00:30 UTC.\nWho is behind it\nTRM attributes this attack to TraderTraitor, North Korea’s premier hacking unit. We base this assessment on robust on and off-chain intelligence sources. The FBI named the same group as responsible for the USD 1.5 billion Bybit hack in February 2025. Several features of this attack match earlier North Korea-linked heists, including the Bybit hack and the KelpDAO hack in April 2026: small Tornado Cash withdrawals funding an operator wallet, staging that began well before the theft, and signatures obtained in advance then executed in one burst. The laundering observed so far, built on large swaps through market makers and lending protocols, differs from the bridge-heavy pattern that followed Bybit. TRM will share further findings as the investigation develops.\nHow much was taken\nTRM traced every transfer from Binance-attributed wallets to attacker-controlled addresses across ten blockchains. Valuing each transfer at the price of the asset at the moment it was taken, the total is approximately USD 6.36 billion. At current prices, the same assets are worth about USD 6.23 billion.\nEthereum accounts for about USD 3.79 billion. That includes 200,630 ETH and 185 other tokens, among them 1.875 billion USDT, 823 million USDC and 88.5 million RLUSD. Bitcoin outflows total 18,270 BTC, about USD 1.47 billion. Tron outflows total 612.7 million USDT and 46.4 million TRX, about USD 628 million. The XRP Ledger lost 233.46 million XRP, about USD 296 million. Solana outflows total 970,500 SOL, about USD 96 million. Arbitrum, Optimism and Base combine to about USD 39 million, including 74.6 million ARB taken on Arbitrum. Avalanche outflows total about USD 28 million, and Polygon outflows total about USD 10 million.\nAn overview of the October 29, 2026 Binance hack in TRM How the attack unfolded\nPreparation began at least 36 hours before the theft. The attacker's operator wallet received three small withdrawals from Tornado Cash, a service that obscures where crypto comes from, starting the morning of October 28. Early on October 29, the operator deployed a first piece of code for the attack and tested it on two wallets. Later that day, it rehearsed the final version on a third wallet, then routed the test funds back to itself. Four hours after that rehearsal, the same code was installed on a Binance hot wallet.\nThe first theft of the night came on the XRP Ledger. Between 20:05 and 20:09 UTC, five Binance wallets sent 233.46 million XRP to a single attacker address, leaving each wallet with only its minimum balance. That XRP has remained in place since. Binance wallets on Solana and Optimism followed shortly after 20:10 UTC. At 20:20 UTC, a Binance Bitcoin hot wallet sent 18,270 BTC to an attacker address, which began splitting the funds into round 1,000 BTC and 100 BTC pieces inside the same block. That technique, called a peel chain, makes funds harder to follow. Within two minutes, a Binance hot wallet on Tron sent 612.7 million USDT and 46.4 million TRX to the attacker.\nHow the attacker took over a Binance hot wallet\nA hot wallet is a wallet connected to the internet that an exchange uses to process customer withdrawals. The Ethereum hot wallet in this case is one of the best-known Binance wallets.\nEIP-7702, introduced in Ethereum's Pectra upgrade in May 2025, allows an ordinary wallet to hand control of its behavior to a smart contract by signing an authorization. From then on, every call to the wallet runs that contract's code, and the arrangement stays in place until the wallet signs a new one.\nAt 20:20 UTC, the operator submitted a single transaction containing 50 authorizations. Each was signed by the Binance hot wallet, and each point"
