   "text": "On Open Doors day, the enigmatic and fascinating Isle of May reveals its history and its stories.\nSometimes the island is a dark mass of brooding mystery. Sometimes it’s a pearl on the horizon, its cliffs gleaming with the droppings from its 200,000 odd sea birds.\nSometimes water and light bends perspective. The island lunges towards the East Neuk coastline, all clear detail, dramatic cliff and white foghorns. Other times it seems to shrink to the horizon, half-obscured by mist and fog. The view of the island is never the same – a constant shifting of light, cloud, water, sun, rock and yes, the reflection of bird shit.\nAt night, its lighthouse rakes the Firth of Forth and North Sea every 15 seconds. On the mainline, on a dark winter’s night the winds howl through the old smuggling wynds and drives the waves at the harbour sea walls. Unperturbed, the lighthouse skims its steady winking beacon like it has done so for so many nights down the years.\nThe Isle of May from the shoreline is Mrs C’s favorite view in the world after St Pauls looming up at the end of Fleet Street in London. The family joke is that we can wrap her up in a bath chair, leave her with her view, and pick her up at the end of the holiday. She loves the sense that the human presence is marginalised – the island is nature’s realm, where nature gets on with its business.\nThe island can look like a wild mystery, a drift into a gothic imagination where it inhabits territory similar to the film, The Lighthouse. A place of isolation where the now silent foghorns relentlessly bellow. The Lighthouse explores repressed sexuality, alcohol abuse and a descent into madness. Its main characters are trapped in the purgatory of endless storms and gulls screaming omens of disaster. Mermaids and supernatural creatures taunt the tortured human interlopers. It doesn’t end well. Sometimes, the Isle of May really does look like the kind of place where a deranged, barely alive Robert Pattinson lies nude on the rocks, writhing in agony as gulls peck at his exposed organs.\nAnd yet, the island in many ways, is just not that isolated or mysterious – its waters are too fertile for fishing, its location is too strategic and key to human interest. Furthermore, the Forth has always been busy with shipping. But it’s also an area of hidden rocks, reefs, sandbanks, sudden fogs or haar, and malignant storms. Shipwrecks and loss of life were common along this stretch of coast.\nNatureScot staff and bird enthusiasts stay on the island during the season. Come November, they leave the island for winter. But the island receives a lot of visitors. During the season, it’s a popular day trip from Anstruther. For an island only a mile long and and half a mile long, it packs in a lot of life and history. And on one day in the year, the island fully opens its doors to reveal its history.\nAn island of pilgrims and saints\nFirst there are the pilgrims and saints. The island had one of the earliest Christian churches in Scotland. The current chapel on the site is dedicated to Saint Adrian of May (sometimes known as Ethernan). He died in AD 875 when marauding Vikings slaughtered the entire population of the monastery. The island was abandoned until the 12th century when David I of Scotland obtained the church again, and the Benedicte community was established. One of the tasks of the monks was to pray for the souls of the Kings of Scots.\nThe island, with its relics of St Adrian, became a popular destination for pilgrims and royal visitors. The shrine could help a woman become pregnant. It could also provide cures; the bones of pilgrims buried on the island showed that most had been seriously ill. The weather-beaten remains of St. Adrian’s medieval chapel and a 12th-century Benedictine priory are still there on the island.\nAn island of war\nUnsurprisingly, with its strategic location, the Isle of May has been an island of war. During World War II, the navy maintained a control centre on the island to detect U-boats and enemy surface vessels trying to enter the Forth\nThe island also lent its name to the Battle of May in 1918.The name is misleading, a classic example of British gallows humour for a terrible event when the Royal Navy attacked itself. A sequence of accidental collisions between Royal Navy warships occurred over little more than an hour which saw two submarines sunk with heavy loss of life, another four damaged along with a light cruiser.\nJudging by the old black and white photos, the island was frequently visited during war. There were prayers and services amongst the ruins of St Adrian’s chapel. There were 7-a-side football matches between armed forces and trawler crews. Wuffy, a beloved naval dog, who drowned was buried with a wooden headstone.\nLow Light lighthouse and research volunteer centre\nFamily life and tragedy at the lighthouse\nLighthouses are remarkable. They are a symbol of our common humanity, rising out of dramatic crashing waves, a coming together to save lives for people we may never meet. As a nation, we may have less connection with the sea than previous generations, but it’s still in our bones. It’s why, along with the Shipping Forecast, harbour walls and old-fashioned trawlers, lighthouses evoke nostalgia and longing. They are a repository for folklore, our history, often macabre stories and feats of engineering.\nThey are also, of course, about bleak human isolation set amongst the relentless power of the seas. It’s easy to be romantic about something when you don’t have to actually do the job. On the sea towers, the lighthouses that emerge straight out of the sea, the keeper couldn’t even go for a walk.\nLife on the lighthouse of the Isle of May seemed to be relatively social and civilised. Robert Stevenson’s lighthouse, built in 1816, is an airy beautiful building, a feat, at the time, of modern lighthouse architecture. There were facilities so that lighthouse keepers could bring their families, there was fishing in a small loch and a strea"
